  "type": "numerical",
  "level": 1,
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>In the given figure of meter bridge experiment, the balancing length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></math> corresponding to null deflection of the galvanometer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. The balancing length, if the radius of the wire <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></math> is doubled, will be _____ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cm</mi></math>.</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/c203dd6c-62ce-4e8e-807f-9f617383c51c-image.png\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 141px;\" /></p>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "40",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>&nbsp;Even if the radius of wire is doubled, the balancing point would not change as<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></math> , which is independent of area in case of uniform wire. Thus, the balancing length remains the same i.e.,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>.</p></blockquote>",
    "image": null
  },
